# The desktop OAuth client identifier, read at runtime by
# `desktop/src-tauri/src/platform/google_auth.rs`.
#
# Untracked rather than committed: it is per-installation configuration, and keeping it out of
# the tree means a fork does not inherit a client it cannot use. It holds an identifier and not
# a secret — a desktop app is a public client and has no client secret at all; PKCE takes its
# place — so this is about ownership rather than confidentiality.
#
# Create it as {"client_id": "<id>.apps.googleusercontent.com"} from an OAuth 2.0 client of
# type "Desktop app" in the Google Cloud Console for project `weakup-remote-2026`. Until it
# exists, sign-in reports exactly that and names where to get one.
oauth_client.json

# Android release signing material. None of this exists in the tree yet, and it
# must stay that way: a keystore is the private half of the identity that says an
# APK came from this developer, and a committed one cannot be un-leaked from a
# public history — the only remedy is generating a new key, which orphans every
# already-installed copy of the app from its update path.
#
# `key.properties` holds the store and key passwords in plain text. The release
# workflow reconstructs both from repository secrets at build time and writes them
# outside the tree, so nothing here ever needs to be tracked.
key.properties
*.jks
*.keystore

# Symbolication related
app.*.symbols

# Obfuscation related
app.*.map.json

# Android Studio will place build artifacts here
/android/app/debug
/android/app/profile
/android/app/release
